#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699482 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\left(1 - x\\right)^{3 y + 1}$, evaluate $f_x(-1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=1 - x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&=-1\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}u^{3 y + 1}&=\\left(3 y + 1\\right)\\,u^{3 y}\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}\\\\\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(u^{3 y} \\left(3 y + 1\\right)\\right)\\cdot\\left(-1\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\left(1 - x\\right)^{3 y} \\left(3 y + 1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=-1$ and $y=1$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=1 - \\left(-1\\right)=2.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(-1,1)&=\\left(u_0^{3 \\left(1\\right)} \\left(3 \\left(1\\right) + 1\\right)\\right)\\cdot\\left(-1\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(32\\right)\\cdot\\left(-1\\right)\\\\\n&=-32.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese derivatives apply for $1 - x>0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699492 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)}$, evaluate $f_y(- \\frac{\\pi}{2},-1)$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $x$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=x y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}&=x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(2 x^{2} y^{2}\\right)&=4 x^{2} y\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(x\\right)+\\left(4 x^{2} y\\right)\\\\\n&=4 x^{2} y + x \\sin{\\left(x y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $y=-1$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\left(-1\\right)=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(- \\frac{\\pi}{2},-1)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(u_0 \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+\\left(- \\pi^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+\\left(- \\pi^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{2} + \\pi\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese derivatives apply for all real $x,y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699494 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\cos^{2}{\\left(- x^{2} + 2 y \\right)}$, evaluate $f_x(2,\\frac{\\pi}{4} + 2)$." :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=- x^{2} + 2 y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&=- 2 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\cos^2(u)&=2\\cos(u)\\cdot(-\\sin(u))\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}\\\\\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)} \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\\\\n&=4 x \\sin{\\left(- x^{2} + 2 y \\right)} \\cos{\\left(- x^{2} + 2 y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=2$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi}{4} + 2$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=- \\left(2\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4} + 2\\right)=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(2,\\frac{\\pi}{4} + 2)&=\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(u_0 \\right)} \\cos{\\left(u_0 \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(-4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(0\\right)\\cdot\\left(-4\\right)\\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese derivatives apply for all real $x,y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699573 :question/problem "Which diagram represents\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=-x\\,\\mathbf i-y\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nat $A=(-2,1)$, $B=(1,2)$, and $C=(2,-2)$?\n\nEvaluate the two components at each point, then place the tail of its vector at that point. All diagrams use the same scale, with one unit per grid interval." :question/worked-solution "Substitute each point into $\\mathbf F(x,y)=-x\\,\\mathbf i-y\\,\\mathbf j$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(-2,1)&=-(-2)\\,\\mathbf i-(1)\\,\\mathbf j=2\\,\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j \\\\\n\\mathbf F(1,2)&=-(1)\\,\\mathbf i-(2)\\,\\mathbf j=-\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j \\\\\n\\mathbf F(2,-2)&=-(2)\\,\\mathbf i-(-2)\\,\\mathbf j=-2\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlace each tail at its point: from $A$ go 2 units right and 1 down; from $B$ go 1 left and 2 down; from $C$ go 2 left and 2 up. The vectors point toward the origin. The correct diagram is shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab38dc8884f5e665d31b40193057d5af1665d1b0d582aca85cad2ee5fee8ed3c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699580 :question/problem "Which diagram represents\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(y-x)\\,\\mathbf i+(x+y)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nat $A=(-2,1)$, $B=(1,1)$, and $C=(2,-2)$? All diagrams use one unit per grid interval." :question/worked-solution "Evaluate both components at each point, keeping their order: the $\\mathbf i$ component is horizontal and the $\\mathbf j$ component is vertical.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(-2,1)&=(1-(-2))\\,\\mathbf i+(-2+1)\\,\\mathbf j=3\\,\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j \\\\\n\\mathbf F(1,1)&=(1-1)\\,\\mathbf i+(1+1)\\,\\mathbf j=2\\,\\mathbf j \\\\\n\\mathbf F(2,-2)&=(-2-2)\\,\\mathbf i+(2+(-2))\\,\\mathbf j=-4\\,\\mathbf i.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the arrow at $A$ goes 3 right and 1 down, the arrow at $B$ goes straight up 2 units, and the arrow at $C$ goes straight left 4 units. A zero component removes motion along that axis; it does not remove the other component. Keep each tail at its labeled point and check all three arrows.\n\n![](images/bb/bbb0d965c7ec72ee8008b34972172af8cc5635df3d963dd27183a8f21b7f416d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699603 :question/problem "Sketch the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=-xy\\,\\mathbf i+x^2\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nat $A=(-2,1)$, $B=(1,-1)$, and $C=(2,-1)$ by completing both tasks. All diagrams use one unit per grid interval.\n\nEnter the coordinates of the arrowhead for each vector when its tail is at the stated point.\n\nArrowhead from $A$: $(${{A x-coordinate}}$,\\,${{A y-coordinate}}$)$\n\nArrowhead from $B$: $(${{B x-coordinate}}$,\\,${{B y-coordinate}}$)$\n\nArrowhead from $C$: $(${{C x-coordinate}}$,\\,${{C y-coordinate}}$)$\n\nChoose the correct diagram below." :question/worked-solution "First evaluate the field, treating $-xy$ as the negative of the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(-2,1)&=-(-2)(1)\\,\\mathbf i+(-2)^2\\,\\mathbf j=2\\,\\mathbf i+4\\,\\mathbf j \\\\\n\\mathbf F(1,-1)&=-(1)(-1)\\,\\mathbf i+1^2\\,\\mathbf j=\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j \\\\\n\\mathbf F(2,-1)&=-(2)(-1)\\,\\mathbf i+2^2\\,\\mathbf j=2\\,\\mathbf i+4\\,\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors all point up and right, so direction alone cannot select a diagram. Their horizontal and vertical displacements must also match: $(2,4)$ at $A$, $(1,1)$ at $B$, and $(2,4)$ at $C$.\n\nThe field value gives a displacement, not the arrowhead's coordinates. Add each displacement to its tail:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA+\\mathbf F(A)&=(-2+2,\\,1+4)=(0,5),\\\\\nB+\\mathbf F(B)&=(1+1,\\,-1+1)=(2,0),\\\\\nC+\\mathbf F(C)&=(2+2,\\,-1+4)=(4,3).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors at $A$ and $C$ have equal components but different tails and arrowheads. The resulting diagram is shown below.\n\n![](images/00/009e26f3566e0a28d8b74b970ed4e18fab0e42079051764e50124cbc43fba278.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699606 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F=(xy^2+yz)\\,\\mathbf i+(x^2y+xz^2)\\,\\mathbf j+(xy+y^2z+xz)\\,\\mathbf k$. Evaluate $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ at $(1,-1,2)$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j+R\\,\\mathbf k$. The determinant expansion keeps the middle cofactor negative:\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i & \\mathbf j & \\mathbf k \\\\ \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial z} \\\\ xy^2+yz & x^2y+xz^2 & xy+y^2z+xz\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate before substituting any coordinates, treating the other variables as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F &=(R_y-Q_z)\\,\\mathbf i-(R_x-P_z)\\,\\mathbf j+(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\ &=\\bigl((x+2yz)-2xz\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i-\\bigl((y+z)-y\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j \\\\ &\\quad+\\bigl((2xy+z^2)-(2xy+z)\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\ &=(x+2yz-2xz)\\,\\mathbf i-z\\,\\mathbf j+(z^2-z)\\,\\mathbf k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $y$ terms cancel in the middle component, and the $2xy$ terms cancel in the last component. Only after taking these derivatives do we substitute the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F)(1,-1,2)&=\\bigl(1+2(-1)(2)-2(1)(2)\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad-2\\,\\mathbf j+(2^2-2)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\ &=-7\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j+2\\,\\mathbf k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699610 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=z^2\\,\\mathbf i+3y\\,\\mathbf j+x\\,\\mathbf k$, what is $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$?" :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j+R\\,\\mathbf k$. The determinant expansion keeps the middle cofactor negative:\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i & \\mathbf j & \\mathbf k \\\\ \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial z} \\\\ z^2 & 3y & x\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate before substituting any coordinates, treating the other variables as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F &=(R_y-Q_z)\\,\\mathbf i-(R_x-P_z)\\,\\mathbf j+(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\ &=(0-0)\\,\\mathbf i-(1-2z)\\,\\mathbf j+(0-0)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\ &=(2z-1)\\,\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the curl is $(2z-1)\\,\\mathbf j$. In particular, differentiating $3y$ with respect to $z$ or $x$ gives zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699615 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F=(xy+z)\\,\\mathbf i+yz\\,\\mathbf j+xz\\,\\mathbf k$. Evaluate $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ at $(2,-1,3)$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j+R\\,\\mathbf k$. The determinant expansion keeps the middle cofactor negative:\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i & \\mathbf j & \\mathbf k \\\\ \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial z} \\\\ xy+z & yz & xz\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate before substituting any coordinates, treating the other variables as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F &=(R_y-Q_z)\\,\\mathbf i-(R_x-P_z)\\,\\mathbf j+(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\ &=(0-y)\\,\\mathbf i-(z-1)\\,\\mathbf j+(0-x)\\,\\mathbf k \\\\ &=-y\\,\\mathbf i+(1-z)\\,\\mathbf j-x\\,\\mathbf k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow evaluate the curl, rather than the original field:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F)(2,-1,3)&=-(-1)\\,\\mathbf i+(1-3)\\,\\mathbf j-2\\,\\mathbf k \\\\ &=\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-2\\,\\mathbf k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699686 :question/problem "![Vector field with arrows drawn to a common scale.](images/0b/0bd8cbd4a845ad62c4d1834a9b6d12675846291b9a1a88b37ad9933151cb75b8.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^3$. Its $z$-component is zero, and it is independent of $z$. View the $xy$-plane from the positive $z$-axis, so positive $z$ points out of the page.\n\nBoth components vary linearly throughout the domain. The horizontal component increases with $y$ at exactly the same rate that the vertical component increases with $x$. The horizontal component does not change with $x$, and the vertical component does not change with $y$.\n\nWhich statements are true everywhere? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Consider both components of the flow around a small paddlewheel with its axle parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\n- The horizontal flow is more rightward above the center than below it. This contributes a **clockwise** turn.\n- The vertical flow is more upward to the right of the center than to the left. This contributes a **counterclockwise** turn.\n\n![The two component fields produce opposite rotational effects.](images/f9/f9eacfb22ef653c9edc981a0c1ce62c6d35fe2d8015b99177e4a68c599912e66.png)\n\nThe stated rates are equal, so these opposite rotational effects cancel exactly. This cancellation occurs around every point, because the rates are constant throughout the domain. The wheel does not spin, even though the arrows vary in direction and length.\n\nTherefore $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\mathbf 0$ everywhere, and $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational. Zero curl has no direction.\n\nSelect only “$\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\mathbf 0$ everywhere.” The nonzero-curl statement, the negative $z$-direction statement, and “$\\mathbf F$ is not irrotational” are all false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699701 :question/problem "![Vector field with arrows drawn to a common scale.](images/2a/2af371cb122ed5519856947c3784d64f5f93dc70189b05b7e364ce616d837153.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^3$. Its $z$-component is zero, and it is independent of $z$. View the $xy$-plane from the positive $z$-axis, so positive $z$ points out of the page.\n\nEvery arrow has the same direction and length, and this uniform pattern continues throughout the domain.\n\nWhich statements are true everywhere? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Imagine a small paddlewheel with its axle parallel to the $z$-axis. The arrows at its upper and lower sides are identical, as are the arrows at its left and right sides. The flow can carry the wheel along, but does not make it spin.\n\nThe comparison below distinguishes unequal sideways pushes (left) from equal sideways pushes (right). The present field has equal pushes, like the right panel.\n\n![](images/59/594106633e422d24b9fea113d24fb6367fdef5ac52724931860e4163e33ce992.png)\n\nThus $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\mathbf 0$ everywhere. A field with zero curl everywhere is **irrotational**. A zero vector has no direction, so it cannot point in the positive $z$-direction.\n\nSelect “$\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\mathbf 0$ everywhere” and “$\\mathbf F$ is irrotational.” Leave the other two statements unselected."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699715 :question/problem "![Vector field with arrows drawn to a common scale.](images/7a/7a955f42458ed8a43ae5748a1e7ee442d328df5ca3e8b85918c31b9dc2ba2a36.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^3$. Its $z$-component is zero, and it is independent of $z$. View the $xy$-plane from the positive $z$-axis, so positive $z$ points out of the page.\n\nAll vectors are horizontal. Their horizontal component increases at a constant rate as $y$ increases and does not change with $x$; this pattern continues throughout the domain.\n\nWhich statements are true everywhere? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Imagine a small paddlewheel with its axle parallel to the $z$-axis. Just above its center the horizontal flow is more rightward than just below. This difference makes the wheel turn **clockwise** as viewed from positive $z$. There is no vertical flow to oppose this rotation.\n\nThe left panel below illustrates this unequal-push effect; the right panel shows equal pushes that would not cause rotation.\n\n![](images/59/594106633e422d24b9fea113d24fb6367fdef5ac52724931860e4163e33ce992.png)\n\nThe constant difference in horizontal flow occurs around every point, so the curl is nonzero everywhere. Wrap your right-hand fingers clockwise: your thumb points into the page, in the **negative $z$-direction**. Since the curl is nonzero, the field is not irrotational.\n\nSelect “$\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere” and “$\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F$ points in the negative $z$-direction everywhere.” Leave the other two statements unselected."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699816 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x^2+xy)\\,\\mathbf i+(x^2y+y^3)\\,\\mathbf j$, what is the scalar curl of $\\mathbf F$?" :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$, the scalar curl is $Q_x-P_y$. In each partial derivative, hold the other variable constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\n&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x^2y+y^3)-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^2+xy)\\\\\n&=2xy-x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe term $y^3$ is constant with respect to $x$, and $x^2$ is constant with respect to $y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699821 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=y e^{xy}\\,\\mathbf i+x\\ln(x+y)\\,\\mathbf j$, defined where $x+y>0$. Evaluate the scalar curl of $\\mathbf F$ at the point $(1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$, the scalar curl is $Q_x-P_y$. Both cross-partials require the product rule and the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\bigl(x\\ln(x+y)\\bigr)\\\\\n&=\\ln(x+y)+\\frac{x}{x+y},\\\\[4pt]\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\n&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\bigl(y e^{xy}\\bigr)\\\\\n&=e^{xy}+xy e^{xy}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_x-P_y&=\\ln(x+y)+\\frac{x}{x+y}-e^{xy}-xy e^{xy}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe point $(1,1)$ lies in the domain because $1+1>0$. Evaluating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(Q_x-P_y)(1,1)&=\\ln 2+\\frac12-e-e\\\\\n&=\\ln 2+\\frac12-2e.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699829 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x^2+y^2)\\,\\mathbf i+\\sin(xy)\\,\\mathbf j$. Evaluate the scalar curl of $\\mathbf F$ at the point $(1,\\pi)$." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$, the scalar curl is $Q_x-P_y$. Differentiate before substituting the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\n&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\sin(xy)-\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^2+y^2)\\\\\n&=y\\cos(xy)-2y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $y$ in the first term comes from the chain rule. At $(1,\\pi)$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(Q_x-P_y)(1,\\pi)&=\\pi\\cos(\\pi)-2\\pi\\\\\n&=-\\pi-2\\pi\\\\\n&=-3\\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699899 :question/problem "Which diagram represents\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)\\,i+\\left(|x|\\right)\\,j\n$$\n\nat $A=(0,-1)$, $B=(0,1)$, $C=(1,-3)$? All diagrams use one unit per grid interval.\n\nEvaluate both components at each point, then place the tail of its vector at that point." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$. Substitute into each component, then combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0,-1)&=-(-1)\\frac{0}{-1}=0 \\\\\nQ(0,-1)&=-(-1)|0|=0 \\\\\n\\mathbf F(0,-1)&=0 \\\\\nP(0,1)&=-(-1)\\frac{0}{1}=0 \\\\\nQ(0,1)&=-(-1)|0|=0 \\\\\n\\mathbf F(0,1)&=0 \\\\\nP(1,-3)&=-(-1)\\frac{1}{-3}=- \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\nQ(1,-3)&=-(-1)|1|=1 \\\\\n\\mathbf F(1,-3)&=-\\frac{1}{3}\\,i+j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first component gives the horizontal displacement and the second gives the vertical displacement. Place each tail at its labeled point. A zero component gives no displacement along that axis; a zero vector stays at its tail.\n\n![](images/3d/3dc1cb0a1fc04e960aa082f3519d346f2eb1229106ed76353cb95fbe4f91deb0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699901 :question/problem "Which diagram represents\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{y}{xy+1}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{x}{xy+1}\\right)\\,j\n$$\n\nat $A=(0,3)$, $B=(1,0)$, $C=(3,-1)$? All diagrams use one unit per grid interval.\n\nEvaluate both components at each point, then place the tail of its vector at that point." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$. Substitute into each component, then combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0,3)&=\\frac{(1)(3)}{(0)(3)+1}=3 \\\\\nQ(0,3)&=\\frac{(1)(0)}{(0)(3)+1}=0 \\\\\n\\mathbf F(0,3)&=3\\,i \\\\\nP(1,0)&=\\frac{(1)(0)}{(1)(0)+1}=0 \\\\\nQ(1,0)&=\\frac{(1)(1)}{(1)(0)+1}=1 \\\\\n\\mathbf F(1,0)&=j \\\\\nP(3,-1)&=\\frac{(1)(-1)}{(3)(-1)+1}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nQ(3,-1)&=\\frac{(1)(3)}{(3)(-1)+1}=- \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\n\\mathbf F(3,-1)&=\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-\\frac{3}{2}\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first component gives the horizontal displacement and the second gives the vertical displacement. Place each tail at its labeled point. A zero component gives no displacement along that axis; a zero vector stays at its tail.\n\n![](images/59/59bf4b515e94e726d3af8a991cfdfd54927e994e664d87a757534bb430ed92c5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699906 :question/problem "Which diagram represents\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(y-2x\\right)\\,i+\\left(-2y+4x\\right)\\,j\n$$\n\nat $A=(1,0)$, $B=(0,0)$, $C=(0,-2)$? All diagrams use one unit per grid interval.\n\nEvaluate both components at each point, then place the tail of its vector at that point." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$. Substitute into each component, then combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(1,0)&=(0)-(2)(1)=-2 \\\\\nQ(1,0)&=(-2)(0)-(2)(-2)(1)=4 \\\\\n\\mathbf F(1,0)&=-2\\,i+4\\,j \\\\\nP(0,0)&=(0)-(2)(0)=0 \\\\\nQ(0,0)&=(-2)(0)-(2)(-2)(0)=0 \\\\\n\\mathbf F(0,0)&=0 \\\\\nP(0,-2)&=(-2)-(2)(0)=-2 \\\\\nQ(0,-2)&=(-2)(-2)-(2)(-2)(0)=4 \\\\\n\\mathbf F(0,-2)&=-2\\,i+4\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first component gives the horizontal displacement and the second gives the vertical displacement. Place each tail at its labeled point. A zero component gives no displacement along that axis; a zero vector stays at its tail.\n\n![](images/8d/8df0c8ea5dc6fe03bf070c2ca8a899715f9b627f11bbb756e8a27cdabdbae663.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699911 :question/problem "Which diagram represents\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(3\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left(-xy\\right)\\,j\n$$\n\nat $A=(0,2)$, $B=(-3,-2)$, $C=(2,-1)$? All diagrams use one unit per grid interval.\n\nEvaluate both components at each point, then place the tail of its vector at that point." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$. Substitute into each component, then combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0,2)&=(3)\\sin(0)=0 \\\\\nQ(0,2)&=(-1)(0)(2)=0 \\\\\n\\mathbf F(0,2)&=0 \\\\\nP(-3,-2)&=(3)\\sin\\left(- \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right)=3 \\\\\nQ(-3,-2)&=(-1)(-3)(-2)=-6 \\\\\n\\mathbf F(-3,-2)&=3\\,i-6\\,j \\\\\nP(2,-1)&=(3)\\sin(\\pi)=0 \\\\\nQ(2,-1)&=(-1)(2)(-1)=2 \\\\\n\\mathbf F(2,-1)&=2\\,j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first component gives the horizontal displacement and the second gives the vertical displacement. Place each tail at its labeled point. A zero component gives no displacement along that axis; a zero vector stays at its tail.\n\n![](images/80/80baba867a547b0c71a308ecf0a051d10ec74e494731d00aac1bd42ea25e8a98.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699914 :question/problem "Which diagram represents\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{2y}{1-y}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{x}{y-1}\\right)\\,j\n$$\n\nat $A=(1,-2)$, $B=(-3,0)$, $C=(0,3)$? All diagrams use one unit per grid interval.\n\nEvaluate both components at each point, then place the tail of its vector at that point." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$. Substitute into each component, then combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(1,-2)&=\\frac{(2)(-2)}{1-(-2)}=- \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\nQ(1,-2)&=\\frac{(1)(1)}{(-2)-1}=- \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n\\mathbf F(1,-2)&=-\\frac{4}{3}\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\,j \\\\\nP(-3,0)&=\\frac{(2)(0)}{1-(0)}=0 \\\\\nQ(-3,0)&=\\frac{(1)(-3)}{(0)-1}=3 \\\\\n\\mathbf F(-3,0)&=3\\,j \\\\\nP(0,3)&=\\frac{(2)(3)}{1-(3)}=-3 \\\\\nQ(0,3)&=\\frac{(1)(0)}{(3)-1}=0 \\\\\n\\mathbf F(0,3)&=-3\\,i\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first component gives the horizontal displacement and the second gives the vertical displacement. Place each tail at its labeled point. A zero component gives no displacement along that axis; a zero vector stays at its tail.\n\n![](images/47/47d19b0ab827362425b410fbb97374b1ff1e5fb615598893489b2c5cc7f61a65.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699941 :question/problem "Given the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\bigl(2 x^{2} + 1\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i-\\bigl(x - 2 z\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j-\\bigl(2 x z - y\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ at $(2,1,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j+R\\,\\mathbf k$. The middle cofactor is negative:\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k \\\\ \\partial/\\partial x&\\partial/\\partial y&\\partial/\\partial z \\\\ 2 x^{2} + 1 & - x + 2 z & - 2 x z + y\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nTake the cross partial derivatives, holding the other coordinates constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R_y-Q_z&=\\bigl(1\\bigr)-\\bigl(2\\bigr) \\\\ &=-1 \\\\ P_z-R_x&=\\bigl(0\\bigr)-\\bigl(- 2 z\\bigr) \\\\ &=2 z \\\\ Q_x-P_y&=\\bigl(-1\\bigr)-\\bigl(0\\bigr) \\\\ &=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F=-\\,\\mathbf i+2 z\\,\\mathbf j-\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nSubstitute the point into these derivatives, not into the original field:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R_y-Q_z&=-1 \\\\ P_z-R_x&=2 (-1) \\\\ &=-2 \\\\ Q_x-P_y&=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n(\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F)(2,1,-1)=-\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-\\,\\mathbf k\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699942 :question/problem "Given the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=2 x y e^{z}\\,\\mathbf j-2 y z e^{x}\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ at $(1,1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j+R\\,\\mathbf k$. The middle cofactor is negative:\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k \\\\ \\partial/\\partial x&\\partial/\\partial y&\\partial/\\partial z \\\\ 0 & 2 x y e^{z} & - 2 y z e^{x}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nTake the cross partial derivatives, holding the other coordinates constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R_y-Q_z&=\\bigl(- 2 z e^{x}\\bigr)-\\bigl(2 x y e^{z}\\bigr) \\\\ &=- 2 x y e^{z} - 2 z e^{x} \\\\ P_z-R_x&=\\bigl(0\\bigr)-\\bigl(- 2 y z e^{x}\\bigr) \\\\ &=2 y z e^{x} \\\\ Q_x-P_y&=\\bigl(2 y e^{z}\\bigr)-\\bigl(0\\bigr) \\\\ &=2 y e^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F=-\\bigl(2 x y e^{z} + 2 z e^{x}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i+2 y z e^{x}\\,\\mathbf j+2 y e^{z}\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nSubstitute the point into these derivatives, not into the original field:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R_y-Q_z&=- 2 (1) (1) e^{(1)} - 2 (1) e^{(1)} \\\\ &=- 4 e \\\\ P_z-R_x&=2 (1) (1) e^{(1)} \\\\ &=2 e \\\\ Q_x-P_y&=2 (1) e^{(1)} \\\\ &=2 e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n(\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F)(1,1,1)=-4 e\\,\\mathbf i+2 e\\,\\mathbf j+2 e\\,\\mathbf k\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699955 :question/problem "Given the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=-2 y z\\,\\mathbf i+2 y^{2}\\,\\mathbf j-2 z^{2}\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ at $(-1,-2,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j+R\\,\\mathbf k$. The middle cofactor is negative:\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k \\\\ \\partial/\\partial x&\\partial/\\partial y&\\partial/\\partial z \\\\ - 2 y z & 2 y^{2} & - 2 z^{2}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nTake the cross partial derivatives, holding the other coordinates constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R_y-Q_z&=\\bigl(0\\bigr)-\\bigl(0\\bigr) \\\\ &=0 \\\\ P_z-R_x&=\\bigl(- 2 y\\bigr)-\\bigl(0\\bigr) \\\\ &=- 2 y \\\\ Q_x-P_y&=\\bigl(0\\bigr)-\\bigl(- 2 z\\bigr) \\\\ &=2 z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F=-2 y\\,\\mathbf j+2 z\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nSubstitute the point into these derivatives, not into the original field:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R_y-Q_z&=0 \\\\ P_z-R_x&=- 2 (-2) \\\\ &=4 \\\\ Q_x-P_y&=2 (1) \\\\ &=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n(\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F)(-1,-2,1)=4\\,\\mathbf j+2\\,\\mathbf k\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699958 :question/problem "Given the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=x y\\,\\mathbf i-\\bigl(2 x + 2 y z\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j-x z\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ at $(-1,1,2)$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j+R\\,\\mathbf k$. The middle cofactor is negative:\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k \\\\ \\partial/\\partial x&\\partial/\\partial y&\\partial/\\partial z \\\\ x y & - 2 x - 2 y z & - x z\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nTake the cross partial derivatives, holding the other coordinates constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R_y-Q_z&=\\bigl(0\\bigr)-\\bigl(- 2 y\\bigr) \\\\ &=2 y \\\\ P_z-R_x&=\\bigl(0\\bigr)-\\bigl(- z\\bigr) \\\\ &=z \\\\ Q_x-P_y&=\\bigl(-2\\bigr)-\\bigl(x\\bigr) \\\\ &=- x - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F=2 y\\,\\mathbf i+z\\,\\mathbf j-\\bigl(x + 2\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nSubstitute the point into these derivatives, not into the original field:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R_y-Q_z&=2 (1) \\\\ &=2 \\\\ P_z-R_x&=(2) \\\\ &=2 \\\\ Q_x-P_y&=- (-1) - 2 \\\\ &=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n(\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F)(-1,1,2)=2\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j-\\,\\mathbf k\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699961 :question/problem "Given the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z)=\\,\\mathbf i-x y z\\,\\mathbf j-2 e^{x + y}\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$ at $(-1,1,0)$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j+R\\,\\mathbf k$. The middle cofactor is negative:\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k \\\\ \\partial/\\partial x&\\partial/\\partial y&\\partial/\\partial z \\\\ 1 & - x y z & - 2 e^{x + y}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nTake the cross partial derivatives, holding the other coordinates constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R_y-Q_z&=\\bigl(- 2 e^{x + y}\\bigr)-\\bigl(- x y\\bigr) \\\\ &=x y - 2 e^{x + y} \\\\ P_z-R_x&=\\bigl(0\\bigr)-\\bigl(- 2 e^{x + y}\\bigr) \\\\ &=2 e^{x + y} \\\\ Q_x-P_y&=\\bigl(- y z\\bigr)-\\bigl(0\\bigr) \\\\ &=- y z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F=\\bigl(x y - 2 e^{x + y}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i+2 e^{x + y}\\,\\mathbf j-y z\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nSubstitute the point into these derivatives, not into the original field:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R_y-Q_z&=(-1) (1) - 2 e^{(-1) + (1)} \\\\ &=-3 \\\\ P_z-R_x&=2 e^{(-1) + (1)} \\\\ &=2 \\\\ Q_x-P_y&=- (1) (0) \\\\ &=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n(\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F)(-1,1,0)=-3\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700124 :question/problem "![Vector field; arrows share one scale.](images/4e/4e9791f4cf71dae512cf952136a5c50e48d9ef03863b9f59d04d8c5109aeb785.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^3$. Its $z$-component is zero and it is independent of $z$. View the $xy$-plane from the positive $z$-axis, so positive $z$ points out of the page. Both planar components are affine functions, and the displayed pattern continues throughout the domain.\n\n\n\nWhich statements are true everywhere? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Imagine a small paddlewheel with its axle parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe horizontal flow is unchanged between the upper and lower sides of a wheel, so it contributes no rotation.\n\nThe vertical flow is unchanged between the right and left sides of a wheel, so it contributes no rotation.\n\nChanges of horizontal flow along the horizontal axis and vertical flow along the vertical axis stretch or compress the flow, but add no paddlewheel rotation.\n\nThe wheel does not spin. Thus $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\mathbf 0$ everywhere, and $\\mathbf F$ is **irrotational**. The zero vector has no direction.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **false**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F$ points in the positive $z$-direction everywhere: **false**.\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational: **true**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **true**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700134 :question/problem "![Vector field; arrows share one scale.](images/24/244e4367f36519c4823a351572643a168d3fe40f49d08cab98918bdc1ad75c31.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^3$. Its $z$-component is zero and it is independent of $z$. View the $xy$-plane from the positive $z$-axis, so positive $z$ points out of the page. Both planar components are affine functions, and the displayed pattern continues throughout the domain.\n\n\n\nWhich statements are true everywhere? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Imagine a small paddlewheel with its axle parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe horizontal flow is more rightward above a wheel than below it. This contributes a **clockwise** turn.\n\nThe vertical flow is more downward to the right of a wheel than to its left. This contributes a **clockwise** turn.\n\nThese rotational effects reinforce each other.\n\nChanges of horizontal flow along the horizontal axis and vertical flow along the vertical axis stretch or compress the flow, but add no paddlewheel rotation.\n\nThe wheel turns **clockwise** everywhere, so $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere. Wrap the fingers of your right hand clockwise: the thumb points into the page, in the **negative $z$-direction**. The field is **not irrotational**.\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is not irrotational: **true**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **true**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **false**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F$ points in the negative $z$-direction everywhere: **true**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700141 :question/problem "![Vector field; arrows share one scale.](images/3f/3f4ee9bfe37893d4ea1ef8261a7d7b9f87f14bc3d48479abc7d7e1269f05219f.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^3$. Its $z$-component is zero and it is independent of $z$. View the $xy$-plane from the positive $z$-axis, so positive $z$ points out of the page. Both planar components are affine functions, and the displayed pattern continues throughout the domain.\n\n\n\nWhich statements are true everywhere? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Imagine a small paddlewheel with its axle parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe horizontal flow is more leftward above a wheel than below it. This contributes a **counterclockwise** turn.\n\nThe vertical flow is more upward to the right of a wheel than to its left. This contributes a **counterclockwise** turn.\n\nThese rotational effects reinforce each other.\n\nChanges of horizontal flow along the horizontal axis and vertical flow along the vertical axis stretch or compress the flow, but add no paddlewheel rotation.\n\nThe wheel turns **counterclockwise** everywhere, so $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere. Wrap the fingers of your right hand counterclockwise: the thumb points out of the page, in the **positive $z$-direction**. The field is **not irrotational**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **false**.\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational: **false**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **true**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F$ points in the negative $z$-direction everywhere: **false**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700147 :question/problem "![Vector field; arrows share one scale.](images/2a/2a67b57192dace6f98c79e76d788cf69678867da4c7bf3c0dd4d6abaa8457e68.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^3$. Its $z$-component is zero and it is independent of $z$. View the $xy$-plane from the positive $z$-axis, so positive $z$ points out of the page. Both planar components are affine functions, and the displayed pattern continues throughout the domain.\n\n\n\nWhich statements are true everywhere? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Imagine a small paddlewheel with its axle parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe horizontal flow is unchanged between the upper and lower sides of a wheel, so it contributes no rotation.\n\nThe vertical flow is unchanged between the right and left sides of a wheel, so it contributes no rotation.\n\nChanges of horizontal flow along the horizontal axis and vertical flow along the vertical axis stretch or compress the flow, but add no paddlewheel rotation.\n\nThe wheel does not spin. Thus $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\mathbf 0$ everywhere, and $\\mathbf F$ is **irrotational**. The zero vector has no direction.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F$ points in the positive $z$-direction everywhere: **false**.\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational: **true**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **false**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **true**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700155 :question/problem "![Vector field; arrows share one scale.](images/ba/baf7781f0913ddd21d4e23b1a9ff523cb8476f28f5edfb4b1f9e00251c1c959f.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^3$. Its $z$-component is zero and it is independent of $z$. View the $xy$-plane from the positive $z$-axis, so positive $z$ points out of the page. Both planar components are affine functions, and the displayed pattern continues throughout the domain.\n\n\n\nWhich statements are true everywhere? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Imagine a small paddlewheel with its axle parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe horizontal flow is more leftward above a wheel than below it. This contributes a **counterclockwise** turn.\n\nThe vertical flow is more upward to the right of a wheel than to its left. This contributes a **counterclockwise** turn.\n\nThese rotational effects reinforce each other.\n\nChanges of horizontal flow along the horizontal axis and vertical flow along the vertical axis stretch or compress the flow, but add no paddlewheel rotation.\n\nThe wheel turns **counterclockwise** everywhere, so $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere. Wrap the fingers of your right hand counterclockwise: the thumb points out of the page, in the **positive $z$-direction**. The field is **not irrotational**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **false**.\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is not irrotational: **true**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F$ points in the positive $z$-direction everywhere: **true**.\n\n- $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F\\ne\\mathbf 0$ everywhere: **true**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700198 :question/problem "Given the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(x^{2} + 6 x + 3 y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(y^{2} + y e^{x}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j$, evaluate its scalar curl at $(0,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$, the **scalar curl** is $Q_x-P_y$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_x&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(y^{2} + y e^{x}\\right)\\\\\n&=y e^{x}\\\\\nP_y&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(x^{2} + 6 x + 3 y^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=6 y\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(y e^{x}\\right)-\\left(6 y\\right)\\\\\n&=y e^{x} - 6 y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate first, then substitute the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(Q_x-P_y)(0,-1)&=\\left(-1\\right)-\\left(-6\\right)\\\\\n&=5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700207 :question/problem "Given the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- x^{2} - x + y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(x - y^{3} - y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j$, evaluate its scalar curl at $(-1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$, the **scalar curl** is $Q_x-P_y$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_x&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(x - y^{3} - y^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=1\\\\\nP_y&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- x^{2} - x + y^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=2 y\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(1\\right)-\\left(2 y\\right)\\\\\n&=1 - 2 y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate first, then substitute the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(Q_x-P_y)(-1,1)&=\\left(1\\right)-\\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700208 :question/problem "Given the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- x^{2} - x - y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- y^{2} + y \\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j$, evaluate its scalar curl at $(-2,1)$." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$, the **scalar curl** is $Q_x-P_y$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_x&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- y^{2} + y \\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{2 x y}{x^{2} + 1}\\\\\nP_y&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- x^{2} - x - y^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 2 y\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(\\frac{2 x y}{x^{2} + 1}\\right)-\\left(- 2 y\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{2 x y}{x^{2} + 1} + 2 y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate first, then substitute the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(Q_x-P_y)(-2,1)&=\\left(- \\frac{4}{5}\\right)-\\left(-2\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{6}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700218 :question/problem "Given the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- 4 x^{2} - 3 y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- y^{2} + \\sin{\\left(x y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j$, evaluate its scalar curl at $(0,\\frac{\\pi}{2})$." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$, the **scalar curl** is $Q_x-P_y$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_x&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- y^{2} + \\sin{\\left(x y \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=y \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)}\\\\\nP_y&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- 4 x^{2} - 3 y^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 6 y\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(y \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)}\\right)-\\left(- 6 y\\right)\\\\\n&=y \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} + 6 y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate first, then substitute the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(Q_x-P_y)(0,\\frac{\\pi}{2})&=\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)-\\left(- 3 \\pi\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{7 \\pi}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700229 :question/problem "Given the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- x^{2} - 3 \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(3 x y + 2 y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j$, defined where $x+y>0$, evaluate its scalar curl at $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$, the **scalar curl** is $Q_x-P_y$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_x&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(3 x y + 2 y^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=3 y\\\\\nP_y&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- x^{2} - 3 \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{3}{x + y}\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=\\left(3 y\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{3}{x + y}\\right)\\\\\n&=3 y + \\frac{3}{x + y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate first, then substitute the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(Q_x-P_y)(0,\\frac{1}{2})&=\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-\\left(-6\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{15}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700422 :question/problem "Given $f(x)=1$ and $F'(x)=f(x)$, find the general expression for $F(x)$. Use $C$ for the arbitrary constant of integration.\n\n$F(x)=$ {{antiderivative}}" :question/worked-solution "We need a function whose derivative is $1$. Since integration reverses differentiation,\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe constant integrand is the power $x^0$, so the power rule applies with $n=0$:\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\int x^0\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{x^{0+1}}{0+1}+C=x+C.\n$$\n\nCheck:\n\n$$\nF'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(x+C)=1+0=1=f(x).\n$$\n\nEvery constant $C$ gives the same derivative, so $x$ alone is only one antiderivative; $x+C$ describes the general family."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700427 :question/problem "Calculate\n\n$$\n\\int x^9\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSelect the general antiderivative, including the arbitrary constant of integration." :question/worked-solution "The power rule for integration increases the exponent by one and divides by that new exponent. With $n=9$,\n\n$$\n\\int x^9\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{x^{9+1}}{9+1}+C=\\frac{x^{10}}{10}+C.\n$$\n\nHere $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^{10}}{10}+C\\right)=\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot 10x^9+0=x^9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700430 :question/problem "Given $f(x)=x^4$ and $F'(x)=f(x)$, a student proposes\n\n$$\nG(x)=\\frac{x^5}{4}+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Check the proposed expression by differentiating it, then give the correct general antiderivative of $f$. Use $C$ for the arbitrary constant.\n\n1. The derivative of the proposed expression is $G'(x)=$ {{derivative}}.\n2. The correct general expression is $F(x)=$ {{antiderivative}}." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the proposed expression first:\n\n$$\nG'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^5}{4}+C\\right)=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 5x^4+0=\\frac{5x^4}{4}.\n$$\n\nThis is not $f(x)=x^4$. The student increased the exponent to $5$ but divided by the old exponent $4$. To undo differentiation, divide by the new exponent:\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\int x^4\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{x^{4+1}}{4+1}+C=\\frac{x^5}{5}+C.\n$$\n\nVerify the correction:\n\n$$\nF'(x)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^5}{5}+C\\right)=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 5x^4+0=x^4=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe arbitrary constant remains in the antiderivative and disappears in its derivative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700563 :question/problem "Given $f(x)=x^{3}$ and $F'(x)=f(x)$, a student proposes\n\n$$\nG(x)=\\frac{x^{3}}{4}+C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the proposed expression, then give the correct general antiderivative of $f$. Use $C$ for the arbitrary constant.\n\n1. $G'(x)=$ {{derivative}}.\n2. $F(x)=$ {{antiderivative}}." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the proposed expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G'(x)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{4}+C\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 3x^{2}+0 \\\\ &=\\frac{3 x^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe proposed expression divides by the new exponent but leaves the exponent unchanged.\n\nIntegration reverses differentiation, so $F'(x)=f(x)$ gives $F(x)=\\int f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nApply the power rule with $n=3$ and include an arbitrary constant $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x)&=\\int x^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\frac{x^{3+1}}{3+1}+C \\\\ &=\\frac{x^{4}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^{4}}{4}+C\\right)&=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4x^{3}+0 \\\\ &=x^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700567 :question/problem "Given $f(x)=x^{8}$ and $F'(x)=f(x)$, a student proposes\n\n$$\nG(x)=x^{9}+C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the proposed expression, then give the correct general antiderivative of $f$. Use $C$ for the arbitrary constant.\n\n1. $G'(x)=$ {{derivative}}.\n2. $F(x)=$ {{antiderivative}}." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the proposed expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G'(x)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(x^{9}+C\\right) \\\\ &=1\\cdot 9x^{8}+0 \\\\ &=9 x^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe proposed expression omits division by the new exponent.\n\nIntegration reverses differentiation, so $F'(x)=f(x)$ gives $F(x)=\\int f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nApply the power rule with $n=8$ and include an arbitrary constant $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x)&=\\int x^{8}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\frac{x^{8+1}}{8+1}+C \\\\ &=\\frac{x^{9}}{9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^{9}}{9}+C\\right)&=\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 9x^{8}+0 \\\\ &=x^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
